          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Grafting denotes Gentile believers being incorporated into the covenant people and blessings rooted in Israel, while natural Jewish branches may be grafted back through renewed faith.\",\"book_summary\":\"Grafting denotes either the Gentile outsider being received into the covenant tree or repentant Israel being accepted back into it.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Incorporation or reincorporation into the covenant people/tree: Gentiles are grafted in, and unbelieving Israel may be grafted back.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Reception into the covenant tree, applying both to Gentiles and to repentant Israel's restoration.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book states the regrafting of Israel as a coordinate case, whereas the consensus foregrounds Gentile inclusion, but the consensus explicitly recognizes both cases.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings identify Paul's olive-tree metaphor in Romans 11 and apply grafting to the same two movements: wild Gentile branches entering the covenant tree and natural Israelite branches being accepted back upon leaving unbelief. The book is terser but does not alter the referent, conditions, or covered cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Because the consensus presents Gentile inclusion as the primary meaning while the book gives Gentile inclusion and Israel's regrafting equal billing, one might call the book a broadening. However, Romans 11:23 and all consensus sets already treat Israel's regrafting as part of the symbol's recognized extension, so this is emphasis rather than substantive reclassification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
